The '''Red Summer''' ({{Langx|bl|Verano Rojo}}; {{Langx|ca|Estiu Roig}}) was a period of left-wing political violence that swept [[Balisca]] from May to September 1917. The violence was characterized by coordinated bombings, assassinations, and arson attacks targeting financial institutions, government agencies, and prominent figures associated with the [[1916 Baliscan banking crisis|banking scandal]] and the broader corruption of the [[Baliscan Gilded Age|Gilded Age]] political establishment. The period represented the most serious outbreak of political violence in Balisca since the [[Cantonal Revolution]] and is widely considered a direct precursor to the [[Baliscan Revolution]] of 1919.

The immediate catalyst for the Red Summer was the federal government's response to the collapse of the [[Banc de Castejón]] in April 1917. The government's decision to bail out wealthy creditors and foreign investors while offering minimal compensation to ordinary depositors sparked outrage, particularly among the [[W:Working-class|working-class]] families that lost their life savings. Radical organizations, particularly anarchist and communist groups that had been organizing underground since the suppression of the labor movement in the previous decade, seized on the crisis to launch a campaign of violence against the institutions and individuals they held responsible.

The Red Summer resulted in at least 134 deaths and over 200 injuries from bomb attacks and assassinations. The government responded with a campaign of repression that saw over 2,000 arrests, the declaration of [[W:Martial law|martial law]] in major cities, and the closure of newspapers and organizations suspected of sympathizing with the attackers. The repression, rather than restoring order, further radicalized the opposition and contributed to the revolutionary sentiment that would erupt following the [[1918 Baliscan confederal election|disputed election of 1918]].

==Background==
{{Main article|1916 Baliscan banking crisis|Baliscan Gilded Age}}

===Onset of the banking crisis===

The collapse of the [[Banc de Castejón]] in April 1917 was the culmination of years of speculative investments, fraud, and regulatory failure. The bank, one of the largest financial institutions in Balisca, had been a cornerstone of the Gilded Age economy, financing [[Rail transport in Balisca|railway construction]], [[Mining in Balisca|mining operations]], and agricultural expansion across the country. Its directors, drawn from the highest levels of the [[Volisan aristocracy]] and closely connected to the [[Partido Liberal Democrático|PLD]] [[W:Political machine|political machine]], had used depositors' funds to finance increasingly risky ventures, assuming that the bank's political connections would protect them from any potential fallout.

When the bank's speculative positions collapsed in early 1917, the full extent of the fraud became apparent. An estimated 300 million [[Baliscan sol|sol]] in depositors' funds had been lost, representing the life savings of hundreds of thousands of working-class families. The bank's directors had known of the [[W:Insolvency|insolvency]] for months but had continued to accept deposits while quietly moving their own funds to safer institutions. Internal documents later recovered showed that the directors had explicitly discussed using their political connections to secure a [[W:Government bailout|government bailout]] while minimizing their personal exposure to losses.

The federal government's response to the crisis was shaped by [[Secretariat of Finance and Civil Service (Balisca)|Finance Secretary]] [[Augusto Paredes]], a former director of the bank who maintained close ties to its current leadership. Paredes orchestrated a bailout package that protected large creditors, including foreign banks and wealthy investors, while offering ordinary depositors only cents on the sol. The government justified this approach by arguing that protecting the financial system required prioritizing institutional creditors, but the contrast with the treatment of small depositors was stark. While foreign banks received full compensation for their losses, families who had entrusted their savings to the bank received only minimal payments stretched over years, amounts that would never make them whole.

===Radical organizing===

The [[labor movement in Balisca]] had been driven underground by the repression of the previous decade. The anti-sedition laws of 1908 and the [[1905 Baliscan constitutional amendments|constitutional changes of 1905]] had effectively outlawed industrial unionism and political organizing by workers, forcing labor activists to operate in secret. Despite official suppression, however, the movement had continued to grow, sustained by the worsening conditions of the Gilded Age and the memory of the [[Cantonal Revolution]].

Anarchist and communist organizations had proven particularly resilient. Drawing on the anarcho-syndicalist tradition that had been strong in Balisca since the late nineteenth century, anarchist groups maintained networks of cells that operated independently but shared a common ideological commitment to the overthrow of the existing order. The [[Confederación Nacional de Trabajadores Baliscanos]] (CNTB), though officially banned from political activity, continued to organize clandestinely, and many of its leaders had concluded that armed struggle was the only path to meaningful change.

The [[Comité de Acción Revolucionaria]] (CAR), which would emerge as the primary organizational force behind the Red Summer violence, was formed in late 1916 by a coalition of anarchist militants, disaffected socialists, and radicalized workers. The group operated in complete secrecy, with cells organized in independent units that had no knowledge of each other's membership or activities. This cellular structure, modeled on revolutionary organizations in Illypnia, made the group extremely difficult for authorities to infiltrate or dismantle.

==Course of events==

===May: The Opening Attacks===

The first major attack of the Red Summer occurred on 18 May 1917, when a bomb exploded at the entrance to the Secretariat of Finance in Castejón. The blast killed three guards and injured fifteen others, including several civilian bystanders. The explosion shattered windows across the plaza and caused structural damage to the building's facade. A manifesto claiming responsibility, signed by the [[Comité de Acción Revolucionaria]], was delivered to several newspapers the following morning. The manifesto denounced the government's handling of the banking crisis and declared that "those who steal from the people will answer to the people" (''Ken roba al pueblo lo responde al pueblo'').

The Ministry of Finance bombing was followed the next days by a series of coordinated attacks across the country. On 21 May, bombs exploded simultaneously at the regional offices of the Ministry of Finance in [[Merced]], [[Macaio]], and [[Axarquía]], killing eight people and injuring dozens. On 24 May, the home of [[Eduardo Feliú]], a director of the Banc de Castejón who had been prominently implicated in the fraudulent schemes, was attacked with incendiary devices. The building was gutted by fire, though Feliú himself was not home at the time. These initial attacks established the pattern that would define the Red Summer: coordinated violence targeting the institutions and individuals associated with the banking scandal and the broader corruption of the Gilded Age.

The government's initial response was one of shock and confusion. The scale and coordination of the attacks caught authorities by suprise, and there was little intelligence about the organizations responsible. President [[Tomás Aldunate]], who had taken office in 1915 following the death of his predecessor, initially attempted to downplay the violence, characterizing it as the work of isolated criminals rather than a organized movement. This approach proved untenable as the attacks continued and intensified.

===June: Letter Bombs and Escalation===

The violence escalated dramatically in June with the introduction of [[W:Letter bombs|letter bombs]] as a tactic. Beginning on 5 June, packages containing explosives were mailed to prominent politicians, corporate executives, newspaper editors, and other figures associated with the [[Baliscan Gilded Age|Gilded Age]] wealthy elites. The campaign was coordinated and systematic, with targets selected for their perceived role in perpetuating the corrupt system.

The first wave of letter bombs targeted members of the [[Confederal Congress]] who had supported the government's handling of the banking crisis. On 8 June, [[Senator Marcos Aberasturi]] was seriously injured when a bomb exploded as he opened his mail at his home in Castejón. Aberasturi lost three fingers on his right hand and suffered extensive burns to his face and chest. The attack on a sitting senator shocked the political establishment and demonstrated that no one, even those in the upper echelons of Baliscan society, were safe from the campaign.

Over the following weeks, letter bombs killed at least eight people and injured dozens more. Among the dead were [[Joaquín Mendizábal]], a prominent newspaper editor for the [[Die Baliskäner]] who had written editorials defending the government's response to the banking crisis; [[Pablo Larrañaga]], a corporate executive who had served on the board of the Banc de Castejón; and [[Father Anselmo Vera]], a conservative priest who had denounced the anarchists from his pulpit. The randomness of the attacks, combined with their lethality, created an atmosphere of fear among the elite. Many wealthy families fled the cities for the relative safety of rural estates, while others hired private security to guard their homes and screen their mail.

The letter bomb campaign was accompanied by continued attacks on physical infrastructure. Arson attacks targeted the offices of corporations implicated in the banking scandal, railway facilities owned by foreign investors, and government buildings associated with tax collection and financial regulation. In some cases, the attackers left behind manifestos or graffiti identifying their motives, making clear that the violence was explicitly political rather than criminal.

===July: The Assassination of Ramón Vilar===

The most dramatic and consequential attack of the Red Summer occurred on 15 July 1917, with the assassination of [[Ramón Vilar]]. Vilar was the powerful boss of the [[Progress Party (Cerdanya)|Progress Party]]'s political machine in Merced, the country's second-largest city. He and his associates had built a political organization that controlled every aspect of life in the city, from employment to housing to the courts, and had used his position to accumulate enormous personal wealth. Vilar had been closely connected to the Banc de Castejón's directors and had personally profited from the bank's operations, receiving loans on favorable terms and insider information about investment opportunities.

On the morning of 15 July, Vilar was leaving a restaurant in central Merced where he had lunched with local business associates. As he walked toward his waiting car, a young man emerged from the crowd and fired three shots from a pistol at close range. Vilar fell to the ground, mortally wounded, while the gunman made no attempt to flee. Bystanders seized the attacker, who was identified as [[Eusebio Molins]], a 24-year-old former bank clerk who had lost his job and his savings when the Banco de Castejón collapsed and the financial crisis began.

Molins reportedly declared at the scene that he had acted to avenge the thousands of families ruined by the bank's collapse. "This man stole from the people and called it business," witnesses quoted him as saying. "I have brought the people's justice." Molins was arrested and taken into custody, but his act had already achieved its intended effect. The assassination of one of the most powerful political figures in Balisca demonstrated that the radical underground could reach even the most protected members of the establishment.

The killing of Vilar sent shockwaves through the political system. For the first time, the violence had claimed the life of a major political figure, and the message was clear: no one was untouchable. Other machine bosses across the country increased their security and limited their public appearances, while wealthy families who had remained in the cities began to leave in large numbers. The government, which had been attempting to manage the crisis through a combination of limited repression and public reassurance, concluded that more drastic measures were necessary.

===August: Government Crackdown===

The government's response to the assassination of Vilar was a sweeping campaign of repression that exceeded anything seen in Balisca since the suppression of the Cantonal Revolution. On 20 July, President Aldunate declared martial law in Castejón, Merced, Macaio, and [[Concepción]], placing the major cities under confederal control, deploying to guard government buildings, banks, and the homes of wealthy citizens, while military tribunals were established to [[W:Trial|try]] those accused of [[W:Political violence|political violence]]. The decision to use the military to defend wealthy interests further inflamed tensions, and disillusionment spread throughout the armed forces.

The anti-sedition laws of 1908, which had been used sporadically against labor organizers, were now invoked systematically to arrest anyone suspected of anti-establishment sympathies. Over the following weeks, more than 1,800 people were detained in raids on [[W:Union hall|union halls]], political organizations, and private homes. Many of those arrested had no connection to the violence but were simply prominent in left-wing circles or had spoken publicly against the government's handling of the banking crisis. The [[Just Society Association]], the [[Christian Socialist Party]], the [[Social Democratic Party (Balisca)|Social Democratic Party]] and other moderate reform organizations were targeted alongside [[Anarchism in Balisca|anarchist]] and [[W:Communism|communist]] groups, their leaders imprisoned and their offices closed.

Newspapers that had reported sympathetically on the grievances underlying the attacks were shut down by government order, the most prominent of them being [[El País]]. Editors and journalists were prosecuted for "inciting disorder" or "providing aid to terrorists," creating a [[W:Chilling effect|chilling effect]] on press coverage of the crisis. The government established an official narrative that portrayed the violence as the work of foreign agitators and criminal elements, rather than a response to genuine grievances. This narrative was enforced through censorship and the prosecution of those who challenged it.

The crackdown, however, proved counterproductive. The heavy-handed repression alienated moderate opinion and drove many who had previously opposed the violence toward sympathy with the radicals. The arrest of respected reform leaders like [[Salvador Mendizábal]] of the Just Society Association and [[W:Cathar Perfect|Cathar Perfect]] [[Father Bartomeu Torres|Bartomeu Torres]] of the Christian Socialist Party convinced many that the government was not interested in addressing grievances but only in protecting the powerful. The labor movement, which had been divided between those who advocated [[W:Reformism|peaceful reform]] and those who supported armed struggle, increasingly united behind the more radical position as it became clear that the government would not tolerate any form of opposition.

===September: Subsiding violence and Trial of Molins===

By September 1917, the immediate wave of violence had begun to subside. The government's crackdown had disrupted the organizational networks of the radical groups, and many key figures had been arrested or had gone deeper underground. The Comité de Acción Revolucionaria, which had coordinated much of the campaign, was infiltrated and ultimately dismantled through arrests of its leadership. However, the underlying grievances that had fueled the violence remained unaddressed, and the radicalization of the opposition continued.

The trial of [[Eusebio Molins]] for the assassination of Ramón Vilar became a focal point for the continuing tensions. Molins was brought before a military tribunal in Merced in early September, in a proceeding that was heavily guarded and largely closed to the public. The government, aware of Molins' growing reputation as a martyr figure, sought to control the narrative by limiting press access and preventing Molins from making political statements.

Despite these restrictions, Molins' trial became a [[W:Cause célèbre|''cause célèbre'']]. Supporters gathered outside the courthouse daily, clashing with police and soldiers who attempted to disperse them. Radical underground newspapers and [[W:Zine|zines]] published accounts of the proceedings that emphasized Molins' motivations and the corruption of his victim. Defense witnesses testified about the suffering caused by the banking collapse and Vilar's role in the corrupt system, effectively turning the trial into an indictment of the Gilded Age order.

Molins was convicted on 28 September 1917 and sentenced to [[W:Death by hanging|death by hanging]]. The sentence was carried out on 5 October, in a execution conducted before dawn at a military facility outside Merced. The government had hoped that Molins' death would end his influence, but instead it cemented his status as a [[W:Martyr|martyr]]. "Molins died for us," became a slogan among radicals, and his name was invoked in the revolutionary struggles that followed. The execution also marked the effective end of the Red Summer as a distinct period, though the violence and repression would continue in various forms until the outbreak of the Baliscan Revolution in 1918.

==Aftermath and legacy==
{{Main|Baliscan Revolution}}

===Political consequences===

The Red Summer had profound and lasting effects on Baliscan politics. The sympathy that was expressed by large swathes of the population for Molis, and the general sense of [[W:Apathy|apathy]] towards those targeted in the attacks demonstrated that significant portions of the population had lost all faith in the existing political system and were willing to use violent means to challenge it. The government's repressive response, rather than restoring order as claimed, had further delegitimized the state in the eyes of many citizens and driven moderate reformers into alliance with radicals.

The labor movement emerged from the Red Summer more united and more militant than ever before. The divisions between reformist and revolutionary factions that had characterized the movement before 1917 were largely mended by the government's heavy-handed decision to target both equally. The [[Socialist Party]], which had previously focused on economic demands and electoral reform, increasingly embraced revolutionary rhetoric that was previously associated with the [[Federación Anárquica del Trabajo]] (FAT) and [[Confederación Autónoma del Trabajo]] (CAT) labor unions. Networks established during the Red Summer, including safe houses, communication channels, and weapons caches, would prove invaluable during the revolution of 1919.

The political establishment, meanwhile, was deeply shaken by the violence. The assassination of Ramón Vilar demonstrated that even the most powerful figures were vulnerable, and many in the elite began to question whether the existing system could survive. Some advocated for meaningful reforms to address the underlying grievances, but they were outnumbered by those who believed that only harsher repression could restore order. The government's response to the Red Summer set the pattern for its approach to the crisis of 1918, when it would again choose repression over reform, with ultimately fatal consequences for the Gilded Age order.

===Historical interpretation===

Historians have debated the significance of the Red Summer in the lead-up to the Baliscan Revolution. Traditional narratives, particularly those written in the immediate aftermath of the revolution, portrayed the violence as the opening phase of the revolutionary struggle, a spontaneous uprising of the people against an unjust system. Later scholarship has emphasized the role of organized radical groups, particularly the Comité de Acción Revolucionaria, in planning and coordinating the attacks, while acknowledging the genuine popular anger that made such organizing possible.

The Red Summer is now generally understood as a critical turning point in Baliscan history. The violence marked the end of any realistic possibility of peaceful reform and the beginning of the final crisis of the Gilded Age order. The government's failure to address the underlying grievances that fueled the violence, combined with its decision to respond with repression rather than reform, created the conditions for the revolutionary upheaval that would follow. The shadow constitution of the [[1883 Confederal Convention]], which had circulated secretly for decades, found new audiences during and after the Red Summer, as radicals sought an ideological framework for the struggle ahead.

The legacy of the Red Summer remains contested in Baliscan politics. For the left, the period is remembered as a heroic, if flawed, resistance to an unjust system, and figures like Eusebio Molins are honored as martyrs in the struggle for justice. For conservatives, the Red Summer represents the dangers of radicalism and the breakdown of social order.

